This paper investigates the normalized ground state solution for the given biharmonic Choquard type equation \begin{align*}
  \begin{split}
  \left\{
  \begin{array}{ll}
  \Delta^2u-\beta\Delta u=\lambda u+\left(\frac{1}{|x|^\mu}*F(u)\right)f(u),
  \quad x\in\mathbb{R}^4,
  \quad u\in H_0^2(\mathbb{R}^4),
  \end{array}
  \right.
  \end{split}
  \end{align*} under specific constraints and growth conditions for a period $T$ and parameter $\beta, \lambda$ within a defined interval. We use the infinite dimensional Sobolev exponent $\frac{4}{3}\;(3< p^{\ast}_2=6)$ and derive the property of the ground state through a direct approach and point-carried fountain theorems. Next, we investigate the positive solution for the given biharmonic Choquard equation \begin{align*}
  \begin{split}
  \Delta^2u-\beta\Delta u=\lambda u+\left(\frac{1}{|x|^\mu}*F(u)\right)f(u),
  \quad x\in\mathbb{R}^4,
  \quad u\in H_0^2(\mathbb{R}^4),
  \end{split}
  \end{align*} under constrained conditions for $\lambda$ and $\beta$ and obtain the high/mid-range multiplicity results. Lastly, we prove the global weak solution for the initial value problem \begin{align*}
  \begin{split}
  u_t=\Delta^2u-\beta\Delta u+\lambda u+\left(\frac{1}{|x|^{\mu}}*F(u)\right)f(u),
  \quad x\in\mathbb{R}^4,
  \quad u\in H_0^2(\mathbb{R}^4),
  \quad t>0,
  \end{split}
  \end{align*} under constrained conditions for $\lambda$ and $\beta$ and time $T$, which yields an $L^p$-bound for solutions. Our proof involves the use of the time dilatation method and $L^p$-bound for solutions.

A method to derive constants of motion beyond local bounds for ODE-based Lagrangian systems is outlined, including a practical example that illustrates its utility for dissipative mechanical systems and its implications on solution existence and behavior under specific assumptions. The framework is further extended to cover Euler-Lagrange equations with order greater than two, yielding new first integrals for particular models, such as the Pais-Uhlenbeck oscillator, paving the way for potential applications in Euler-Lagrange partial differential equations in the future. Lastly, the relationship between these nonlocal constants of motion and energy-dependent bounds is explored. Due to technical restrictions, the discussion is constrained to the class of Lagrangian models dependent on a single variable and its derivative. Consequently, the derived results exhibit simplified characteristics. We anticipate that further development of this approach will enable its application to more complex systems. 

Keywords: Conserved quantities, dissipative systems, boundness of solutions, Euler-Lagrange equations, Pais-Uhlenbeck oscillator, Euler-Lagrange partial differential equations. 

This work is a extension of previously published research that appeared in Journal of Physics A: Mathematical and Theoretical, Volume 55, Number 1, 2022. 

